            <ArticleTitle>A wound-healing program is hijacked to promote cancer metastasis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2813-2815</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1084/jem.20171556</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this issue of JEM, Sundaram et al. (https://doi.org/10.1084/jem.20170354) report a mechanism by which the normal epithelial wound healing response is &quot;hijacked&quot; to promote invasion and metastasis in head and neck squamous carcinomas (HNSCCs), a finding that unveils new markers of poor outcomes and potential targets for therapeutic intervention.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2017 Ellisen.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
